    "prompt": "You are extracting the numeric claims of one section of a model-validation report, so that each\ncan be checked against the artifact it cites. Section: outcomes.\n\nThe prose is printed below one line per line, each prefixed with its own number and a vertical\nbar. List EVERY number that appears in the prose, in the order it appears, including numbers that\nappear inside a citation's logical name and numbers written with a thousands separator or a per\ncent sign. For each one:\n\n- \"line\" is the number printed to the left of the line the number appears on. Give the number\n  only; do not copy the line's text back, and do not count the prefix itself as a number.\n- \"value\" is the number as written, with thousands separators and the per cent sign removed:\n  \"3,500\" is 3500, \"22.0%\" is 22.0.\n- \"unit\" is \"percent\" when the number is written with a per cent sign, \"count\" for a number of\n  rows, features or events, \"currency\", \"bp\" for basis points, \"months\" for a horizon, and\n  \"ratio\" for every other dimensionless quantity (AUC, PSI, VIF, a coefficient, a lift).\n- \"metric\" is what the number measures, taken from the logical name of the artifact it cites\n  (auc, brier, psi, vif, calibration_slope, delta_auc, coefficient, event_rate, ...), or null.\n- \"split\" is train, test, out_of_time or vintage_holdout, or null.\n- \"comparison\" is \"eq\" for a number that equals its artifact; \"delta\" for a difference written\n  \"from a to b\" or \"a gap of x\", whose value is the second cited artifact minus the first; and\n  \"ratio\" for a number written as one artifact divided by another. A delta or ratio carries TWO\n  adjacent citations, in that order.\n- \"citation\" is the [[art:...]] token the number carries, copied exactly, or null when the\n  sentence carries none. Do not invent a citation and do not move one from another number.\n- \"rounding\" is the number of decimals the prose wrote when it wrote fewer than the artifact has\n  (2 for a lift written 2.24), or null.\n\nReturn every number even when it has no citation: a number you leave out is still counted, and\ncounted against the report.\n\nProse:\n 1| **Discrimination is acceptable and, unusually, stable.** From [[art:03ed7db3:run.metrics]]: AUC is 0.7892 (train), 0.7758 (test), 0.7743 (vintage holdout) and 0.7898 (out-of-time), with Gini of 0.578, 0.552, 0.549 and 0.580 and KS of 0.454, 0.428, 0.453 and 0.437. The largest train-to-holdout AUC gap is 0.0149, well inside any conventional degradation threshold; the out-of-time AUC is marginally *above* train. There is no evidence of overfitting in the rank-ordering sense. Interpreted cautiously, however, this stability is also consistent with the split-contamination concern in section 3: if loans are shared across partitions, out-of-sample discrimination will look artificially like in-sample discrimination. The clean result should not be relied upon until the split integrity question is closed.\n 2| \n 3| **Calibration fails, severely, on every split including train.** Mean predicted monthly hazard against observed event rate:\n 4| \n 5| | Split | n | mean predicted | observed event rate | ratio | CPR predicted | CPR actual |\n 6| |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n 7| | train | 36,013 | 0.03925 | 0.00852 | 4.61x | 0.3815 | 0.0976 |\n 8| | test | 15,382 | 0.03802 | 0.00806 | 4.72x | 0.3720 | 0.0926 |\n 9| | vintage_holdout | 32,177 | 0.02582 | 0.00482 | 5.36x | 0.2694 | 0.0563 |\n10| | out_of_time | 12,257 | 0.07793 | 0.01795 | 4.34x | 0.6223 | 0.1953 |\n11| \n12| The reported CPR figures are exactly the annualisation of the mean hazards (for train, 1 − (1 − 0.03925)^12 = 0.3815; for the actuals, 1 − (1 − 0.00852)^12 = 0.0976), so the CPR gap is not an independent error — it is the same level bias expressed on the scale that matters for valuation. The model predicts a 38% annual CPR on its own training data where 9.8% was realised, and a 62% CPR out-of-time against 19.5% realised. A CPR error of this size is disqualifying for MSR valuation: prepayment speed drives the runoff of the servicing fee strip almost one-for-one, and a model running four times fast will undervalue the asset dramatically and will misdirect any hedging built on its durations.\n13| \n14| That the bias is present *in the training sample* rules out the usual explanations — regime shift, population change, or holdout mis-specification — and points instead to a defect in the fitting or scoring path. A correctly fit hazard model with an intercept reproduces the sample base rate almost exactly. Candidate causes worth investigating: predictions emitted on a different scale or from a different link than the one used in fitting; an offset, exposure, or annualisation applied to the scored output but not to the fitted target; case-control or event-weighted sampling in training that was never corrected back to the population prior; or a mismatch between the loan-month base of `n` and the base over which predictions were averaged.\n15| \n16| **The reported calibration slopes are internally inconsistent with this bias and should not be relied on.** [[art:03ed7db3:run.metrics]] reports slopes of 1.015 (train), 0.954 (test), 0.856 (vintage holdout) and 1.066 (out-of-time). A model over-predicting the mean by a factor of 4.6 cannot simultaneously have a calibration slope of 1.015 unless the slope is being computed in a way that absorbs the level error — for instance as a logistic recalibration slope with a freely refitted intercept, which measures only the shape of the relationship and is blind to level bias by construction. The slope statistic as reported therefore conceals the principal defect, and the vintage-holdout value of 0.856 additionally falls outside a conventional [0.9, 1.1] acceptance band, indicating shape error on that partition as well.\n17| \n18| Brier scores (0.0104 train, 0.0099 test, 0.0061 vintage holdout, 0.0240 out-of-time) and log-loss (0.0630, 0.0612, 0.0416, 0.1174) are inflated relative to what the event rates would support and are consistent with the level bias rather than reassuring against it.\n19| \n20| **Effective challenge.** No challenger model, benchmark, or naive baseline appears in the artifact store. There is consequently no evidence that the fitted hazard specification outperforms a simple seasoning-ramp-plus-incentive baseline, and no basis on which to assess whether the observed AUC of ~0.78 is good, adequate, or poor for this collateral.\n\n\nAnswer with one JSON object and nothing else: no prose before or after it, no explanation, no markdown fence. It must validate against this JSON schema:\n{\"$defs\": {\"Comparison\": {\"description\": \"What the claimed value is asserted to equal.\\n\\n``delta`` and ``ratio`` are cited by two adjacent ``[[art:...]]`` tokens, in order (D-012).\\nThe enum is not nullable: ``eq`` is the explicit default, so \\\"this number equals its artifact\\\"\\nhas one spelling and not two (D-018).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    eq: The value matches the cited artifact.\\n    delta: The value matches ``second - first``.\\n    ratio: The value matches ``second / first``.\", \"enum\": [\"eq\", \"delta\", \"ratio\"], \"title\": \"Comparison\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"ExtractedClaim\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"One claim as the model returns it: the grammar minus the fields the caller already knows.\\n\\n``text``, ``section``, ``id`` and ``source`` are not asked for. The caller knows which section\\nit passed in, the id is a hash of the other three fields, the source of an extraction is\\nalways ``report``, and the text is the line the model named -- which the caller has in front\\nof it, so asking for it back costs output tokens and adds a way for the answer to disagree\\nwith the prose (D-063, D-085).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    line: The 1-based number of the line the number appears on, as the prompt printed it.\\n    value: The number as written, separators and per cent sign removed.\\n    unit: How it is written.\\n    metric: What it measures, or ``None``.\\n    split: Which split it is about, or ``None``.\\n    comparison: ``eq``, ``delta`` or ``ratio``.\\n    citation: The citation the number carries, or ``None``.\\n    rounding: Decimals the prose used, or ``None``.\", \"properties\": {\"line\": {\"minimum\": 1, \"title\": \"Line\", \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"value\": {\"title\": \"Value\", \"type\": \"number\"}, \"unit\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Unit\", \"default\": \"ratio\"}, \"metric\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Metric\"}, \"split\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/SplitName\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"comparison\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Comparison\", \"default\": \"eq\"}, \"citation\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Citation\"}, \"rounding\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Rounding\"}}, \"required\": [\"line\", \"value\"], \"title\": \"ExtractedClaim\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"SplitName\": {\"description\": \"The splits a claim may be stated on, exactly as ``CLAIMS_SCHEMA.json`` enumerates them.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    train: The development split.\\n    test: The held-out split.\\n    out_of_time: A later-period split; hazard subjects.\\n    vintage_holdout: One origination year held out; hazard subjects.\", \"enum\": [\"train\", \"test\", \"out_of_time\", \"vintage_holdout\"], \"title\": \"SplitName\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"Unit\": {\"description\": \"How a number is written, which decides how it is normalised before matching.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    ratio: Every dimensionless quantity not written with a ``%`` -- AUC, PSI, VIF, a\\n        coefficient, a lift.\\n    percent: Written with a ``%``; normalised against a unit-interval artifact by dividing\\n        by 100.\\n    count: A number of rows, features or events. Matched to half a unit, so integers match\\n        exactly.\\n    currency: A money amount.\\n    bp: Basis points, as a rate shock is written.\\n    months: A horizon.\", \"enum\": [\"ratio\", \"percent\", \"count\", \"currency\", \"bp\", \"months\"], \"title\": \"Unit\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"The JSON object the extractor is asked for: a list of claims under one key.\\n\\nA bare JSON array is a shape more models get wrong than a single-key object, and\\n:func:`~quaestor.llm.structured.structured` validates a ``BaseModel``, not a list.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    claims: The claims, in the order the numbers appear.\", \"properties\": {\"claims\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/ExtractedClaim\"}, \"title\": \"Claims\", \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"title\": \"ExtractedClaims\", \"type\": \"object\"}",
